    "result_text": "The build is green. Here's what I implemented for the `namesakeCount` feature.\n\n## Summary\n\nThe requirement: when creating an owner, compute and return `namesakeCount` \u2014 the number of *existing* owners (before this create) that share the same first and last name, case-insensitively. Since the value is read back later via `GET /api/owners/{id}`, it must be persisted on the owner at creation time.\n\nI mirrored the codebase's existing pattern for derived-at-registration fields (`customerCode`, `householdId`):\n\n1. **`Owner` model** (`model/Owner.java`) \u2014 added a persisted `namesakeCount` field (`@Column(name = \"namesake_count\")`) with getter/setter, alongside the existing derived fields.\n\n2. **`NamesakeCounter`** (`service/NamesakeCounter.java`) \u2014 a new small component with a single responsibility, modeled on `CustomerCodeGenerator`. It reuses the existing `OwnerRepository.findByLastNameIgnoreCase` query and `TextNormalizer.normalizeForComparison` (the same case/whitespace canonicalization the household-duplicate rule uses) to count owners matching both names \u2014 no query duplication.\n\n3. **`ClinicServiceImpl.saveOwner`** \u2014 set `namesakeCount` for new owners only (the transient owner isn't yet persisted, so it correctly counts *existing* owners), inside the existing `isNew()` branch next to the customer-code assignment.\n\n4. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 added a read-only `namesakeCount` integer to the `Owner` schema, so the generated `OwnerDto` gains the field and MapStruct auto-maps it from `Owner.getNamesakeCount()` (same as `customerCode`).\n\n5. **Schemas** (`db/hsqldb/schema.sql`, `db/h2/schema.sql`) \u2014 added the `namesake_count INTEGER` column.\n\nThe acceptance test `coreCountsNamesakes` passes (two owners sharing name \u2192 second reports `namesakeCount = 1`), and the full suite is green (`BUILD SUCCESS`, Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0).",
